{
  "an3.100:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 3.100 ",
  "an3.100:0.2": "10. A Lump of Salt ",
  "an3.100:0.3": "A Lump of Salt ",
  "an3.100:1.1": "“Mendicants, suppose you say: ",
  "an3.100:1.2": "‘No matter how this person performs a deed, they experience it the same way.’ This being so, the spiritual life could not be lived, and there’d be no chance of making a complete end of suffering. ",
  "an3.100:1.3": "Suppose you say: ",
  "an3.100:1.4": "‘No matter how this person performs a deed, they experience the result as it should be experienced.’ This being so, the spiritual life can be lived, and there is a chance of making a complete end of suffering. ",
  "an3.100:1.5": "Take the case of the individual who does a trivial bad deed, but it lands them in hell. ",
  "an3.100:1.6": "Meanwhile, another individual does the same trivial bad deed, but experiences it in this life, so that not even a little remains, let alone a lot. ",
  "an3.100:2.1": "What kind of individual does a trivial bad deed, but it lands them in hell? ",
  "an3.100:2.2": "An individual who hasn’t developed their physical endurance, ethics, mind, or wisdom. They’re small-minded and mean-spirited, living in suffering. ",
  "an3.100:2.3": "That kind of individual does a trivial bad deed, but it lands them in hell. ",
  "an3.100:3.1": "What kind of individual does the same trivial bad deed, but experiences it in this life, so that not even a little remains, let alone a lot? ",
  "an3.100:3.2": "An individual who has developed their p